    "howToCelebrate": "For Catholics in the United States wondering how to celebrate First Friday practically, start with Mass — the summit and source of Christian life. While not a Holy Day of Obligation on the U.S. calendar, attending Mass is the most fitting way to honor the feast when your parish schedule allows. Arrive early to pray, sing the hymns fully, and receive Communion with intention. At home, read the day's Gospel as a family and discuss one sentence that struck you. Morning Mass and evening adoration on First Fridays Consider fasting or abstinence if the season calls for it (Lent, Advent penance, or Friday discipline). Share the meaning of the day with children using simple language — feasts teach faith better than lectures. If you cannot attend in person, many parishes livestream major solemnities; still make the day distinct from ordinary time through prayer and gratitude.",
    "holyDayAndMassObligation": "First Friday Devotion is not a Holy Day of Obligation in the United States, but it remains spiritually significant within the Ordinary Time season. Many Catholics choose to attend Mass, pray a novena, or observe customs associated with the day even without canonical obligation. Because this feast is moveable, confirm the date each year through your parish or the USCCB liturgical calendar. School and parish catechists often build lessons around this date; participating reinforces the Church year rhythm for children and adults alike.",
    "whyItMatters": "Why does First Friday Devotion still matter in the twenty-first century? Because secular calendars offer holidays without holiness, while the Church offers feasts that unite earth and heaven. First Friday Masses surged in American parishes during the 20th century. The devotion complements the Sacred Heart solemnity without replacing Sunday obligation. In a culture of constant distraction, fixed and moveable feasts interrupt ordinary time and demand attention to God. Nine First Fridays for the Love of Christ — the heart of this celebration — speaks to struggles Catholics actually face: grief, hope, guilt, joy, fear of death, longing for mercy. Returning to this date each liturgical cycle is not repetition for its own sake; it is formation. Every year the same mysteries, yet the believer is different — older, perhaps wounded or healed — and the feast meets them where they are. That is why Catholic feast days remain among the most searched religious topics in English: people hunger for meaning, and the Church answers with dates that remember God's saving acts.",
